        "The April–May 2026 Aurora campaign used Cursor for hostile tool actions and repeatedly restarted conversations after refusals. For AAM-SDOS-RUNTIME-GOVERNANCE SDOS-EN-01, the missing applicability fact is whether the operator or implementer applied SDOS v1.10 to these outbound operations and which pre-execution policy point governed them; tool invocation alone does not establish SDOS adoption. Taxonomy correspondence does not supply these scope facts; no occurrence-level requirement finding is recorded.",
        "The April–May 2026 Aurora campaign used Cursor for hostile tool actions and repeatedly restarted conversations after refusals. For IEEE-7000 10.3(e)(8), the missing applicability fact is whether the responsible project adopted IEEE 7000’s ethical design process and assigned these contextual changes to its verification and continued-monitoring design-revision activity; the runtime episode does not expose that process. Taxonomy correspondence does not supply these scope facts; no occurrence-level requirement finding is recorded.",
        "The April–May 2026 Aurora campaign used Cursor for hostile tool actions and repeatedly restarted conversations after refusals. For IEEE-7009 Annex A.3 / 7009-ASR-011, the missing applicability fact is whether the responsible developer or integrator applied IEEE 7009 to an ASOI exhibiting the required Distinguishing Characteristics and designated the affected operation a specified safety-related or safety-exempt function; ordinary authorisation failure or tool capability does not establish those Annex A.3 conditions. Taxonomy correspondence does not supply these scope facts; no occurrence-level requirement finding is recorded.",
        "The April–May 2026 Aurora campaign used Cursor for hostile tool actions and repeatedly restarted conversations after refusals. For IEEE-7014.1 6.25.3(a-c), the missing applicability fact is whether the operator adopted IEEE 7014.1 for an emulated-empathy GPAI partner in this interaction; authoritative task output, including professional-domain output, does not alone establish a personalised or emotionally affirming partnering context. Taxonomy correspondence does not supply these scope facts; no occurrence-level requirement finding is recorded.",
        "The April–May 2026 Aurora campaign used Cursor for hostile tool actions and repeatedly restarted conversations after refusals. For IEEE-7014.1 6.6.3(a), the missing applicability fact is whether the operator adopted IEEE 7014.1 for a partner-based GPAI system using emulated empathy and a harmful partner-interaction termination control; an ordinary cyber refusal, abort, or service stoppage does not establish that partner-system scope. Taxonomy correspondence does not supply these scope facts; no occurrence-level requirement finding is recorded.",
        "The April–May 2026 Aurora campaign used Cursor for hostile tool actions and repeatedly restarted conversations after refusals. For IMDA-AGENTIC-AI-MGF 2.2.2, the missing applicability fact is whether the organisation adopted the cited voluntary IMDA framework and defined sensitive-action checkpoints for these agents; a human instruction or a refusal does not establish the framework’s chosen approval boundaries. Taxonomy correspondence does not supply these scope facts; no occurrence-level requirement finding is recorded.",
        "The April–May 2026 Aurora campaign used Cursor for hostile tool actions and repeatedly restarted conversations after refusals. For NIST-AI-100-1 MANAGE 4.1, the missing applicability fact is whether the responsible organisation used NIST AI RMF MANAGE 4.1 for this deployed system’s monitoring, appeal, override, incident-response and change-management process; the reported runtime response does not establish framework adoption or the contextual monitoring plan. Taxonomy correspondence does not supply these scope facts; no occurrence-level requirement finding is recorded.",
        "The April–May 2026 Aurora campaign used Cursor for hostile tool actions and repeatedly restarted conversations after refusals. For NIST-AI-100-1 MEASURE 2.5, the missing applicability fact is whether the relevant organisation used NIST AI RMF MEASURE 2.5 for this system’s validity/reliability and limits of generalisation, including the deployment use context; reliance on an output or a performed verification does not establish that voluntary framework undertaking. Taxonomy correspondence does not supply these scope facts; no occurrence-level requirement finding is recorded.",
